## Getting Started

### Prerequisites
- Python 3.11+
- `poetry` (or `pip-tools`) for dependency management
- Git

### Setup

```bash
git clone https://github.com/<org>/constellai.git
cd constellai
poetry install
pre-commit install
```

### Verify your environment

```bash
pytest tests/physics_validation/
```

This must pass before any other work begins — it validates the SGP4 propagator against Vallado's published test vectors and cross-checks against an independent library (Skyfield or poliastro). Every downstream result depends on this being correct.

---

## Roadmap

Progress is tracked via milestone checklists below and mirrored as [GitHub Issues](../../issues) / [Milestones](../../milestones).

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 0 — Environment & Repo Setup</strong></summary>

- [ ] Create folder skeleton (see `docs/adr/0001-repo-structure.md`)
- [ ] Set up Python environment (pinned version, `poetry`)
- [ ] Install pre-commit hooks (`black`, `ruff`, `mypy`)
- [ ] Configure GitHub Actions: lint + type-check + unit test on every PR
- [ ] Set up experiment tracking (MLflow or W&B)
- [ ] Each dev confirms a reproducible environment (`docs/setup.md`)

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 1 — Literature Review & Problem Formalization</strong></summary>

- [ ] Survey existing conjunction-assessment methods (covariance-based, TLE-only)
- [ ] Survey temporal/dynamic GNN literature for trajectory/interaction forecasting
- [ ] Survey MARL for coordination under physical/fuel constraints (MAPPO, QMIX, MADDPG, COMA)
- [ ] Write up problem definition, gap analysis, explicit contribution statement
- [ ] Mentor review before modeling begins

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 2 — Orbital Mechanics Core (M1) + Simulator (M5)</strong></summary>

- [ ] TLE parsing and SGP4 propagation
- [ ] Validate against Vallado test vectors — **hard gate**
- [ ] Cross-validate against Skyfield or poliastro
- [ ] Coordinate/frame transforms (ECI, ECEF, RTN)
- [ ] J2, drag, and solar radiation pressure perturbation models
- [ ] Conjunction geometry (TCA, miss distance) and error propagation
- [ ] Independent scenario generator in `simulation/` (no shared code paths with the model pipeline)
- [ ] Physics validation test suite

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 3 — Non-ML Baseline</strong></summary>

- [ ] Miss-distance/TCA-threshold conjunction flagging baseline
- [ ] Run end-to-end on real TLE data
- [ ] Record baseline metrics before any ML training

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 4 — Graph Construction (M2) + TGNN Prototype (M3)</strong></summary>

- [ ] Define graph schema (node features, edge criteria, temporal windowing)
- [ ] Implement graph construction pipeline from M1 outputs
- [ ] First TGNN prototype — sanity-check by overfitting a toy subset
- [ ] ADR justifying TGNN architecture vs. alternatives
- [ ] Train on real data, compare vs. Step 3 baseline with confidence intervals

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 5 — MARL Environment (M4)</strong></summary>

- [ ] Define MDP/POMDP formulation (state, action, reward, observation model)
- [ ] Build RL environment on M1/M5 (not M3 yet)
- [ ] Single-agent baseline policy on toy 2–3 satellite scenario
- [ ] ADR justifying algorithm choice (MAPPO or QMIX first)
- [ ] Scale to multi-agent training; compare vs. independent single-agent baselines

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 6 — Integration (M3 → M4)</strong></summary>

- [ ] Feed TGNN forecasts into MARL state/reward per frozen interface contract
- [ ] Full pipeline, end-to-end, multi-day scenario, no manual intervention
- [ ] Red-team session: critique the integrated system as conference reviewers would

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 7 — Scalability & Sensitivity Analysis</strong></summary>

- [ ] Measure degradation as satellite count increases
- [ ] Identify where centralized MARL breaks down; validate a decomposition strategy (hierarchical/neighborhood-based control)
- [ ] Ablation studies: temporal component, physics constraints, reward terms
- [ ] Sensitivity analysis on hyperparameters and physical assumptions

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Step 8 — Paper & Capstone Prep</strong></summary>

- [ ] Draft methods, results, figures, tables
- [ ] Mentor red-team review of full draft
- [ ] Finalize reproducibility (every number traceable to config + commit hash)
- [ ] Prepare capstone demonstration

</details>

